# Line endings, decided once for every tool and OS.
#
# The repository stores LF everywhere. Without this file, Windows Git
# (core.autocrlf=true) checks files out as CRLF, and any tool that doesn't
# share that setting (editors, WSL, other git binaries) then sees every file
# as modified. That was the "line-ending churn" in the audit diffs.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts keep CRLF in the working tree. Windows PowerShell 5.1
# and cmd are the only consumers. The .ps1 files also carry a UTF-8 BOM,
# which PowerShell 5.1 needs to read non-ASCII characters correctly; git
# leaves the BOM alone.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.gz binary
*.zip binary
*.apk binary
*.aab binary
*.jar binary
